<Base
  title="Honor tied to home and duty"
  description="Iliad Books 6 and 9: Hector and Andromache, shame as a reason to stand, and Achilles refusing the embassy’s gifts."
>
  <LessonChrome slug="honor-and-home">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>Honor tied to home and duty</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Today we add duty to the people a man protects. The layer is still honor.
        It now has a house standing on it.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="learn" aria-labelledby="will-learn">
      <h2 id="will-learn">What you’ll learn</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Hector’s courage is for the people he protects. Andromache asks him to stay. He will not, because of how the Trojans would see him.</li>
        <li>The course’s name for that motive is aidōs, shame. Butler’s English is “with what face should I look upon the Trojans.”</li>
        <li>The embassy offers Achilles gifts. He refuses. He says coward and hero are held in equal honour.</li>
        <li>Honor tied to duty toward others is steadier than honor that is only about oneself. Achilles’ refusal is the second kind, still unhealed.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <div class="lesson-body">
      <p class="scene-today">Today we add duty to the people a man protects to the foundation.</p>
      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>Two men who will not meet</h3>
      <p>
        Let’s begin in the space between the armies. Glaucus and Diomedes step out
        to fight, and Diomedes asks who the other man is. Glaucus answers that men
        come and go as leaves. He then tells the story of Bellerophon, and of his
        father Hippolochus, who sent him to Troy with a charge: fight among the
        foremost, and do not shame the blood of his fathers. Diomedes finds that
        their grandfathers exchanged gifts. They plant their spears, pledge
        friendship, and exchange armour. The poem says Glaucus takes leave of his
        wits, gold for bronze. Notice what has happened to honor. It is still a
        public worth. It is also a tie between houses, older than this war.
      </p>

      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>Hector at the gate</h3>
      <p>
        Hector goes looking for Andromache and finds her at the Scæan gate, with
        the nurse and the child. The people call the boy Astyanax, because his
        father stands alone as chief guardian of Ilius. Andromache asks Hector to
        stay on the wall. She has already lost father, mother, and brothers.
        Achilles killed them. Hector is what she has left.
      </p>
      <p>
        Notice his answer. He has thought of all of it. He asks with what face he
        should look upon the Trojans, men or women, if he shirked battle like a
        coward. He knows only how to fight in the forefront and win renown for his
        father and himself. He knows the city will fall. He grieves for none of
        that as he grieves for her, the day some Achæan takes her freedom and sets
        her to a loom in Argos. The course’s word for the feeling that keeps him
        outside the wall is <em>aidōs</em>, shame: the inability to meet the eyes
        of his own people. Butler does not print the Greek. He prints the face,
        and the coward.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then he tells her to go inside, to the loom and the distaff and the
        servants, “for war is man’s matter, and mine above all others of them that
        have been born in Ilius.” The Greek of those lines is under the English in
        the reader. His courage serves the people he protects, and it also sends
        him away from them. That is the cost of tying honor to a house. It does
        not make the man stay. It tells him why the staying would shame him, and
        whom the standing is for.
      </p>

      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>The gifts, and the refusal</h3>
      <p>
        Book 9 is the other test. The Greeks are losing. Nestor says Agamemnon
        dishonoured a hero whom heaven itself had honoured, and that they must
        appease Achilles with presents and fair speeches. Agamemnon offers a great
        list: gold, horses, women, a daughter, seven cities. Phoenix, Ajax, and
        Ulysses go. They find Achilles singing the feats of heroes, with Patroclus
        sitting opposite him.
      </p>
      <p>
        Achilles’ answer to Ulysses is the sentence you should be able to say
        back. He has no thanks for all his fighting. He that fights fares no
        better than he that does not. Coward and hero are held in equal honour.
        He will not be moved by Agamemnon. He tells Phoenix not to weep for the
        king’s sake. He has honour from Jove, he says, which will abide at his
        ships while he has breath. Ajax says a man will take a fine even for a
        dead brother, and that Achilles is implacable about one girl. Achilles
        likes the speech better, and still refuses. He will not fight until Hector
        reaches the Myrmidon ships.
      </p>
      <p>
        Set the two scenes together. Hector’s honor is fastened to a wife, a
        child, and a city that can see his face. Achilles’ honor, in this book, is
        still the wound of Lesson 2: a prize taken, and a public slight. Gifts do
        not heal it, because the worth he cares about was other men’s opinion, and
        that opinion has been made equal for the coward and the brave. Honor rooted
        in duty to others is the steadier of the two. You have now seen both.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you work among other people, you already know the difference. A duty
        you can name — the patient, the client, the household — holds when
        applause does not. A slight to your standing, with no one you are bound to
        protect in the room, can keep you out of the work. Book 9 is that second
        case, still inside the first layer. A city and a law are not here yet.
      </p>
    </div>

