    <section class="word-day" aria-labelledby="word-day">
      <h2 id="word-day">Word of the day</h2>
      <p class="word-greek" lang="grc">αἰδώς</p>
      <p>
        <em>aidōs</em>, shame, in this course’s sense: the reluctance to be seen
        failing the people who know you. Hector’s sentence is the example. “With
        what face should I look upon the Trojans, men or women, if I shirked
        battle like a coward?” Say <em>aidōs</em> once. Then find “face” and
        “coward” in Book 6.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Reveal
      items={[
        {
          prompt: "Why do Glaucus and Diomedes stop their fight?",
          answer:
            "Their houses are already guest-friends. Diomedes’ grandfather entertained Bellerophon. They exchange armour and agree to avoid each other’s spears.",
        },
        {
          prompt: "Why will Hector not stay on the wall, though Andromache asks him?",
          answer:
            "He asks with what face he could look at the Trojans if he shirked like a coward. He fights in the forefront for his father’s renown and his own, and he grieves most for what will happen to her.",
        },
        {
          prompt: "What does Achilles tell Ulysses about the coward and the hero?",
          answer:
            "Coward and hero are held in equal honour, and death deals like measure to the man who works and the man who is idle. He will not take the gifts.",
        },
        {
          prompt: "Which of the two honors does this lesson call the steadier?",
          answer:
            "Honor rooted in duty to other people, the kind Hector states at the gate. Achilles’ refusal is still honor about his own wounded standing.",
        },
      ]}
    />

    <section class="reflection" aria-labelledby="reflect-title">
      <h2 id="reflect-title">A short reflection</h2>
      <p>
        One short note, if you want it: where has a duty to a particular person
        held you to a task that reputation alone would have let you leave? It
        stays in this browser.
      </p>
      <label class="reflection-label" for="note-home">Your note</label>
      <textarea id="note-home" data-note="honor-and-home" rows="4"></textarea>
    </section>

    <section class="takeaways" aria-labelledby="takeaways-title">
      <h2 id="takeaways-title">Key takeaways</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Lesson 2 laid honor as public worth, and showed how easily it becomes wrath. This lesson ties that worth to a house.</li>
        <li>Glaucus and Diomedes stop because their fathers’ houses already exchanged gifts. Honor can be a bond between houses, not only a prize.</li>
        <li>Hector’s aidōs keeps him in the forefront for the city that can see him, and it takes him from the wife and child he names.</li>
        <li>Achilles, offered gifts, says the coward and the hero are held equal. Duty to others is not yet what moves him. Loyalty, in the next lesson, will.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="summary">
        Honor about one’s own standing can be refused with gifts still on the
        table. Honor fastened to people one protects is harder to put down, and
        it can send a man out to die for them. Both are still the first layer.
      </p>
    </section>

